<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/team-laptops.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/team-laptops-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/team-laptops.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal process</span></nav><p class="kicker kicker-light">Process guide</p><h1>How Insurance Appraisal Works in Connecticut</h1><p class="phero-lede">Eight stages, from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-light" href="/contact-ct-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Insurance appraisal is a short, structured process written into most Connecticut property policies. It answers one question, how much a covered loss is worth, and it answers it with a binding number. This guide walks through each stage in the order it usually happens, with the decisions you will face along the way.</p>
<h2>Before you start: is appraisal the right tool?</h2>
<p>Appraisal works when the insurer agrees the loss is covered but you disagree about the cost. It is not designed to resolve outright denials, disputes over whether an exclusion applies, or claims that the insurer acted unfairly. Those questions usually belong with a Connecticut attorney. If you are unsure, start with an <a href="/ct-appraisal-services/insurance-estimate-review/">estimate review</a>.</p>
<h2>Stage 1: Pin down the disagreement</h2>
<p>Write down exactly what is in dispute. Is it the number of roof squares, whether flooring can be matched, the price of plaster repair, or the value of contents? A clear list makes the rest of the process faster and keeps the panel focused.</p>
<h2>Stage 2: Read the appraisal condition</h2>
<p>Connecticut’s standard fire policy is set out in state statute, and most homeowners and commercial forms include an appraisal condition patterned on it. Read yours closely. Note who can demand appraisal, whether the demand must be in writing, how long each side has to name an appraiser (often 20 days), and how the umpire is chosen and paid.</p>
<h2>Stage 3: Send the written demand</h2>
<p>The demand should identify the policy and claim number, state that you disagree on the amount of loss, and invoke appraisal under the policy. Send it in a way you can prove, and keep a copy. If your insurer demands appraisal first, the same deadlines apply to you.</p>
<h2>Stage 4: Name the appraisers</h2>
<p>Each side picks its own appraiser. Policies usually require that person to be competent and disinterested, which generally rules out anyone with a stake in the repair contract. Tell the other side who your appraiser is within the deadline.</p>
<h2>Stage 5: Select the umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers choose a neutral umpire, ideally early, before positions harden. If they cannot agree within the time the policy allows, either party can usually ask a court to appoint one.</p>
<h2>Stage 6: Inspect, compare and negotiate</h2>
<p>The appraisers inspect the property, often together, exchange estimates and work through differences line by line. This is where most disputes narrow. Agreed items are documented so they don’t reopen later.</p>
<h2>Stage 7: Submit open items to the umpire</h2>
<p>Items the appraisers cannot resolve go to the umpire. Each appraiser submits their position with support. The umpire may inspect the property and meet with both appraisers before deciding.</p>
<h2>Stage 8: Sign the award and get paid</h2>
<p>An award signed by any two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss. A well-drafted award shows replacement cost and actual cash value separately so depreciation holdback can be recovered later. Payment then follows the policy’s terms, minus the deductible and any prior payments.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep an eye on other deadlines</b>Appraisal does not automatically extend the time to file suit or to complete repairs for depreciation recovery. Track those dates separately with our <a href="/ct-claim-tools/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a>.</div>
<h2>How long does it take?</h2>
<p>Simple residential appraisals often finish six to ten weeks after both appraisers are named. Large commercial losses, contents-heavy claims and panels that need an umpire hearing can take several months.</p>
<h2>What does it cost?</h2>
<p>Each side pays its own appraiser, and the umpire’s fee is usually split evenly. Ask for fee terms in writing before you sign. Our <a href="/ct-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> helps you see whether the numbers work.</p>
</article>
